# ⛔ DISABLED UNTIL THIS REPOSITORY GOES PUBLIC.
#
# Every owner entry below is commented out on purpose, so this file assigns
# NOBODY and GitHub auto-requests review from NOBODY. Re-enabling is
# uncommenting the three rules at the bottom.
#
# WHY IT IS OFF
#
# CODEOWNERS auto-requests a review from every named owner on every pull
# request that touches a matching path. Opening the first PR here therefore
# mailed four people about a repository that is not ready for them to look at.
# The roster is correct; the timing was wrong.
#
# WHAT TURNING IT OFF COSTS TODAY: NOTHING.
#
# Measured 2026-08-26, and it is the point the original note below anticipated:
#
#     gh api /repos/Frisian-MCP/frisian-mcp-demo/rules/branches/main   -> (empty)
#     gh api /repos/Frisian-MCP/frisian-mcp-demo/branches/main/protection
#         -> 404 Branch not protected
#
# `main` carries no ruleset and no classic protection, so "Require review from
# Code Owners" was never enabled and this file has never required an approval
# of anything. It was documentation with a mailing list attached. Commenting it
# out removes the mailing list and leaves the documentation.
#
# ⚠️ THE CONVERSE, WHICH MATTERS MORE THAN THIS FILE
#
# An unprotected `main` also means the publish control described in
# common/docs/PUBLISHING.md is not in place. That runbook says branch
# protection is what makes "a reviewed merge is the only way to publish" true,
# because the workflow publishes on `push: branches: [main]`. With `main`
# unprotected, anyone with write access can push straight to it. That is latent
# rather than live right now — the publish job also needs the
# FRISIAN_MCP_SPEC repository variable, which is unset, so a merge currently
# builds and verifies and publishes nothing — but it should be closed before
# that variable is ever set.
#
# ── RE-ENABLING, WHEN THE REPOSITORY GOES PUBLIC ────────────────────────────
#
#   1. Create the ruleset on `main` with "Require review from Code Owners".
#      Without it, uncommenting the rules below restores the emails and still
#      requires nothing.
#   2. Uncomment the three rules at the bottom of this file.
#   3. Confirm no owner was silently dropped:
#
#          gh api /repos/Frisian-MCP/frisian-mcp-demo/codeowners/errors
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Everything below is the original file, preserved verbatim so the reasoning
# and the exact roster survive rather than living in git history.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Review gate for the paths that can publish to GHCR.
#
# These globs are deliberately THE SAME SET as the `paths:` filter on the
# `push: branches: [main]` trigger in .github/workflows/build-nautobot.yml.
# A change that can cause a publish is a change that needs a review, and
# keeping the two lists identical is what makes that sentence true rather
# than aspirational. If you add a path to one, add it to the other.
#
# This file is only half the control. It names who must approve; it does not
# require the approval. That comes from a ruleset on `main` with "Require
# review from Code Owners" enabled. Without it, this file is documentation.
#
# ⚠️ WHY INDIVIDUALS AND NOT @Frisian-MCP/core-maintainers
#
# A team that has no access to this repository is IGNORED in CODEOWNERS --
# silently. No error on the PR, no owner assigned, and the "require Code
# Owner review" rule finds nobody to require. Measured 2026-08-25:
#
#     gh api /repos/Frisian-MCP/frisian-mcp-demo/teams  ->  (empty)
#
# The five people below reach the repo through the organisation's base
# permission, not through a team grant, so a team entry here would name a
# group that does not formally have access and would be dropped.
#
# To switch to the team -- worth doing, because a roster in a file goes stale
# -- grant @Frisian-MCP/core-maintainers write on this repository FIRST, then
# replace the handles below with @Frisian-MCP/core-maintainers, and confirm
# with:
#
#     gh api /repos/Frisian-MCP/frisian-mcp-demo/codeowners/errors
#
# That endpoint is the check. Do not switch and assume.

